./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:597:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_012dWuByvFthfkPZ5xS5UtsP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013h33HXF8JPQmJW1Fsfdbgi","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T27.md","content":"# T27 — Phase 4 — Task intent classifier\n\n**Phase:** 4\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R26\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine), [§Ranking Complexity](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#ranking-complexity)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the first stage of the Phase 4 retrieval pipeline: a deterministic, feature-based classifier that reads a user task statement and assigns it one or more intent labels (debug, refactor, explain, add-feature, test, docs, migration, performance, review, etc.). The classifier is the input to every later retrieval stage — anchor extraction biases toward intent-relevant token shapes, candidate retrieval weights source mix by intent, and the scorer applies a `task-type compatibility` signal that requires this label to exist. Per spec §Ranking Complexity, the classifier must stay feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy is layered on top, so that ranking remains explainable for the lifetime of Phase 4.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Pipeline: 1. Parse user task and classify intent.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"Ranking signals: task-type compatibility, graph proximity to anchors, exact identifier match, semantic similarity, verification status, freshness, scope, evidence strength, contradiction/supersession state, past usefulness, recent successful reuse, user preference compatibility, token cost.\" (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)\n\n> \"Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: task intent classifier; anchor extractor and resolver; hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events; scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness; compact response shaper; diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons; benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks.\" (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)\n\n> \"Risk: ranking becomes hard to reason about. Control: keep ranker feature-based and inspectable before any learned policy. Expose diagnostic scores.\" (spec §Ranking Complexity)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R26 (Phase 3 memory-graph review verdict) — ensures retrieval can rely on typed memory streams, link tables, and verification status fields existing as designed.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` — current workflow tools that today perform implicit intent inference inline; T27 lifts that into a dedicated module.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/query/engine.rs` — current heuristic intent detection in the query path; behaviour to preserve and extend.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/lib.rs` — module registry that must expose the new `retrieval_v1` namespace.\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, single source of truth — no parallel intent inference helpers); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (Markdown heading references in code comments where behaviour is doc-anchored).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md` §Retrieval Engine pipeline step 1, §Phase 4 deliverables, and §Ranking Complexity in full so the inspectability constraint is internalized verbatim.\n2. Enumerate the intent labels the classifier must emit, drawn from spec language and current workflow tool coverage: `debug`, `refactor`, `explain`, `add_feature`, `modify_feature`, `add_test`, `update_docs`, `migration`, `performance`, `review`, `unknown`. Document each label with a short doc comment naming the workflow tools that depend on it.\n3. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/mod.rs` (new directory) that re-exports the classifier API and registers the module with `lib.rs`.\n4. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs`: a pure-function classifier `classify_intent(task_text: &str) -> IntentClassification` that returns a primary label, optional secondary labels, per-feature scores, and the literal features that fired (lemma matches, imperative-verb hits, error-keyword hits, file/symbol token shapes, question-form hits). Features must be a finite, documented list — no learned weights, no opaque model — so the result is fully inspectable per §Ranking Complexity.\n5. Surface a `diagnostics()` view on `IntentClassification` that returns `(label, contributing_features, score)` tuples for use by the diagnostic mode of T30 and the working-memory inspector of T36; this is the contract that lets the ranker explain why a label was chosen.\n6. Migrate the existing heuristic intent inference in `query/engine.rs` to call the new classifier instead of duplicating logic; remove the old inline heuristic in the same commit per \"single source of truth\" in `coding.md`.\n7. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent_tests.rs` covering: every label appears for at least one canonical task statement; ambiguous tasks return multiple labels with documented tie-break; empty/whitespace input returns `unknown` without panic; long input is truncated at a documented boundary without losing the leading verb; diagnostics output round-trips through serde.\n8. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings in the new module before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs` — feature-based intent classifier with documented label set and diagnostics view.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for label coverage, ambiguity handling, edge inputs, diagnostics serialization.\n\n(Implicitly required to make the module compile: `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/mod.rs` with module declarations, registered from `lib.rs`.)\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs` — confirms the classifier file exists at the expected path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib retrieval_v1::intent_tests` — runs the in-tree tests and asserts every documented label and edge case is covered.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (`wc -l daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent.rs daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/intent_tests.rs`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. The classifier must use early returns and table-driven feature evaluation; no nested match pyramids.\n- Schema parity: not applicable (no SQL in this task).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: the legacy heuristic intent inference in `query/engine.rs` is removed in this commit; no parallel classifier remains.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent.\n- Doc citation: where the classifier comments explain a behaviour mandated by `## Ranking Complexity` in the plan doc, the comment cites that exact heading per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` \"Markdown Heading References\".\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 4 deliverable \"task intent classifier\" punted to a later phase.\n- [ ] Legacy inline intent heuristic in `query/engine.rs` migrated to the new module in the same commit (single source of truth).\n- [ ] Diagnostics view is exported and exercised by a test, ready to be consumed by T30 (scoring) and T36 (working-memory inspector).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":187,"cache_read_input_tokens":45959,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":187,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"7b3a9aaf-37d5-4241-8bbe-3572b8a62729","request_id":"req_011Cb7MkGmZvqNoB2E9TvBXh","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:621:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013ZXuTanJH7X6D7LHKK4aiQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016cwUC7KSJW4BjPVj93cGrq","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R05.md","content":"# R05 — Foundation review — Phase 0 decisions and baselines\n\n**Phase:** Review for Phase 0\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T01, T02, T03, T04\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 0: Fork Foundation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-0-fork-foundation)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard, definition-of-done checklist.\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis review certifies that Phase 0 has produced an evidence-backed fork/extend decision, a complete architecture overview, a binding crate boundary plan and migration policy, and a runnable baseline benchmark suite. It is the gate that all Phase 1+ tasks depend on; passing it means later phases can rely on a fixed substrate vocabulary and an objective regression baseline.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every artifact produced by T01, T02, T03, T04:\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md`\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md`\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md`\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-crate-boundary-plan.md`\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-storage-migration-policy.md`\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/baseline_workflows.rs`\n  - `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/baselines/baseline_metrics.json`\n  - `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/baselines/README.md`\n- Spec sections this phase claims to satisfy: `## Phase 0: Fork Foundation`, `## Design Thesis`, `## System Architecture`, `## Storage Design`, `## Fork Strategy`.\n- Shared standards: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (hard limits, no broken windows, schema parity policy for future phases) and `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` (every applicable item must be satisfied or explicitly waived).\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Open `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md` and verify it evaluates all four spec gate conditions with explicit `met`/`unmet` verdicts citing files and lines; verify a branch or repo name is committed under the `## Branch or repo name` heading.\n2. Open `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` and verify it contains `## Workspace graph`, `## Event log`, and `## Memory graph` sections; spot-check that each enumerates the node/edge/event/memory families from spec §System Architecture rather than paraphrasing.\n3. Open `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` and verify every existing MCP tool (cross-check against `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` registrations) has a classification of `stable`, `additive`, `redesigned-with-shim`, or `deprecated-with-deadline`. Verify the `## Backward compatibility` section states the binding rule.\n4. Open `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-crate-boundary-plan.md` and verify all seven new substrates (identity, events, memory, retrieval, working-memory, consolidation, verification) are present by literal grep, each with a layout decision and rationale.\n5. Open `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-storage-migration-policy.md` and verify `## Migration order` enumerates Phase 1 through Phase 7 schema introductions in chronological order, and `## Rollback` lists an inverse operation per migration.\n6. Open `daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/baseline_workflows.rs` and verify benches exist for `prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `search_symbols`, and `find_relevant_tests`. Run `cd daemon && cargo bench --bench baseline_workflows -- --warm-up-time 1 --measurement-time 3` and confirm `baseline_metrics.json` is regenerated.\n7. Run `wc -l` on every file produced by Phase 0 to confirm none exceeds 800 lines.\n8. Grep all Phase 0 artifacts for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, and commented-out code blocks; flag any hit as a finding.\n9. Cross-check that doc citations use exact `## Heading` references (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References) and not vague \"see the spec\" prose.\n\n## Required sections in the review output file\n\nThe reviewer writes to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R05-foundation.md` with these mandatory headings (each must contain real findings, not \"n/a\"):\n\n- `## Spec alignment` — phase-by-phase coverage matrix for Phase 0 deliverables: fork decision, architecture docs, crate/module plan, storage migration policy, compatibility policy, baseline benchmarks. One row per deliverable, evidence column citing the artifact and heading.\n- `## Coding-standard alignment` — line-by-line audit vs. hard limits: file length (`wc -l` results per file), forbidden tokens grep, suppression audit, naming audit on the bench function names.\n- `## Findings` — issues found, severity (`blocker`, `major`, `minor`), suggested follow-up tasks. Follow-up tasks must be opened in this build (do not defer outside).\n- `## Verdict` — `PASS`, `PASS-WITH-FOLLOWUP-TASK-Txx`, or `BLOCK` (with named blockers).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R05-foundation.md` — confirms the review markdown exists where downstream tasks can read it.\n- `grep -q '## Spec alignment' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R05-foundation.md` — confirms spec alignment is a dedicated section, not prose.\n- `grep -q '## Coding-standard alignment' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R05-foundation.md` — confirms standards audit was performed under a dedicated heading.\n- `grep -q '## Findings' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R05-foundation.md` — confirms findings are explicit and not buried.\n- `grep -q '## Verdict' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R05-foundation.md` — confirms the review concludes with a binding verdict.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Review markdown file exists at the path verification commands check.\n- [ ] Every required section above is present with concrete content.\n- [ ] If verdict is BLOCK, the blocking issues are listed and a follow-up task is opened in this build (never deferred to a future session).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":615,"cache_read_input_tokens":49571,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":615,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"0f1bc3de-7993-48de-bd17-e18a2fa6e8b8","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mcwaxi2NndD8b7oC78","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:631:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015Pnh5gggscokdtYCqGBG6x","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# T02 — Phase 0 — Successor architecture overview + compatibility policy\n2\t\n3\t**Phase:** 0\n4\t**Type:** docs\n5\t**Model class:** balanced\n6\t**Depends on:** T01\n7\t**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 0: Fork Foundation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-0-fork-foundation)\n8\t**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n9\t\n10\t## Goal\n11\t\n12\tThis task produces the two foundational design documents that govern every later phase: the successor architecture overview (workspace graph, event log, memory graph as three first-class sources of truth) and the MCP compatibility policy (which existing tools remain stable, which gain additive fields, which are deprecated with shims). It matters because spec §Design Thesis requires all three substrates to share identity, storage, and ranking primitives — without a single overview doc, each phase risks diverging. The compatibility policy is binding for every Phase 8 tool redesign and protects existing assistant clients while the substrate evolves.\n13\t\n14\t## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n15\t\n16\t> \"The correct abstraction is not memory attached to a code graph. The correct abstraction is a workspace cognition substrate with three first-class sources of truth: 1. Workspace graph: files, symbols, docs, tests, dependencies, routes, schemas, build targets, runtime surfaces, and ownership boundaries. 2. Event log: every assistant action, tool call, context bundle, edit, diagnostic, test result, user correction, deployment step, and outcome. 3. Memory graph: durable typed claims over the workspace and event history, with evidence, freshness, contradiction, supersession, use history, and retrieval explanations.\"\n17\t\n18\t> \"Phase 0 Deliverables: ... architecture docs copied and revised ... compatibility policy for existing MCP tools.\"\n19\t\n20\t## Inputs\n21\t\n22\t- Dependency outputs: `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md` from T01 (decides the branch/repo target for these docs).\n23\t- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n24\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n25\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n26\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n27\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n28\t  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n29\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` (current 43 registered tools)\n30\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` (current workflow tools)\n31\t- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (no broken windows, single source of truth, citation discipline); `AGENTS.md` \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\".\n32\t\n33\t## Steps\n34\t\n35\t1. Read T01's decision doc and the spec sections §Design Thesis, §System Architecture, §MCP Surface, §MCP Tool Contract Principles.\n36\t2. In `2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md`, write a `## Workspace graph` section enumerating the node and edge families from spec §Workspace Graph and mapping each to the existing or planned crate.\n37\t3. Write a `## Event log` section summarizing the 20 event types, the append-only invariant, compaction snapshots, and the hot-path budget (≤5ms P99) from spec §Event Log and §Phase 2 DoD.\n38\t4. Write a `## Memory graph` section covering the memory classes, link types, evidence model, and the `CounterMemory` distinction from spec §Memory Graph.\n39\t5. Add a `## Shared substrate primitives` section explaining how identity, storage, and ranking are shared across the three substrates.\n40\t6. Add a `## Reference to existing docs` section linking forward to the prior `2026-04-11-*` architecture docs without duplicating their content.\n41\t7. In `2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md`, enumerate every existing MCP tool by name and assign each a status: `stable` (no breaking change), `additive` (gains optional fields only), `redesigned-with-shim` (legacy name keeps responding with a compatibility shim), or `deprecated-with-deadline`.\n42\t8. Write a `## Backward compatibility` section stating the binding rule: every existing assistant client must continue to function for a full phase cycle after any tool's redesign lands. Include the shim removal protocol.\n43\t9. Cross-link the two docs and reference the spec heading anchors precisely.\n44\t\n45\t## Files expected\n46\t\n47\t- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — high-level architecture overview anchoring the three substrates and shared primitives for every later phase.\n48\t- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` — per-tool stability classification and the backward-compatibility rule that binds Phase 8 redesign.\n49\t\n50\t## Verification\n51\t\n52\t- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the architecture doc exists.\n53\t- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` — confirms the compatibility policy exists.\n54\t- `grep -q '## Workspace graph' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the workspace graph substrate is documented under its own heading.\n55\t- `grep -q '## Event log' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the event log substrate is documented under its own heading.\n56\t- `grep -q '## Memory graph' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md` — confirms the memory graph substrate is documented under its own heading.\n57\t- `grep -q '## Backward compatibility' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` — confirms the backward-compatibility rule is stated under a dedicated heading rather than implied.\n58\t\n59\t## Coding-standard checks\n60\t\n61\t- File length ≤800 lines per file (verify with `wc -l docs/architecture/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-architecture.md docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md`).\n62\t- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — not applicable (docs task).\n63\t- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n64\t- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions.\n65\t- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming — applies to any pseudo-signatures shown in the docs.\n66\t- For schema changes: not applicable (no schema changes in this task).\n67\t- Doc citation: cite the exact `## Design Thesis`, `## System Architecture`, and `## MCP Surface` headings from the spec; reference `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-*.md` headings rather than duplicating content (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` § Markdown Heading References).\n68\t\n69\t## Definition of done\n70\t\n71\t- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n72\t- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n73\t- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n74\t- [ ] No deferred work: no item from spec §Phase 0 punted to a later phase.\n75\t- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the architecture doc was updated in the same task.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"8176b57f-8fee-494d-b55e-cffaa1f6f7f0","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:34:18.997Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
